    slug: 'medicare-prescription-payment-plan-activation-fee-scam',
    description:
      'A caller claiming to represent Medicare or a Part D drug plan contacts a beneficiary about the Medicare Prescription Payment Plan (M3P) — a real, voluntary option created by the Inflation Reduction Act that lets Part D enrollees spread their out-of-pocket drug costs, capped at $2,100 for 2026, into monthly installments instead of paying at the pharmacy counter — and falsely claims an upfront "processing" or "activation" fee must be paid over the phone to qualify for the new cap or to enroll in the payment plan. Because the fee pitch is wrapped around a genuinely new and widely publicized benefit change, and because the caller often already has enough information to sound credible, victims are pressured into reading off a Medicare number, Social Security number, or bank details before they have time to check whether the call is real. The New York StateWide Senior Action Council\'s Senior Medicare Patrol program named this "the Medicare Fraud of the Month" for January 2026, warning that "no legitimate Medicare program will ask for payment before benefits can be accessed" and noting that both the $2,100 annual cap and enrollment in the optional payment plan are automatic and free — Medicare never requires a special card, a phone activation, or any fee to access either one. Anyone contacted this way should hang up and call 1-800-MEDICARE directly to confirm their own account status, never give a Medicare or Social Security number to an unsolicited caller, and report the call to their state\'s Senior Medicare Patrol or to the FTC at ReportFraud.ftc.gov.',
