    slug: 'maine-jbrh-excavation-jake-brown-contractor-fraud',
    description:
      'Jake Brown, 35, of Palermo, Maine, ran JBRH Excavation and Welding/Fabrication, LLC — and a related company, GGB Property Maintenance, LLC — taking on excavation, foundation, and general contracting jobs across central Maine and requiring clients to pay a substantial deposit or the bulk of the contract price before work began. A Bangor Daily News investigation published in October 2024 documented a pattern going back to at least 2022: Brown would collect payment, then either abandon the project mid-job, do defective work that had to be redone by someone else, or never show up at all, leaving at least 13 identified clients — including a Litchfield housing developer out $278,000, a Troy homeowner who lost wrongful-death settlement money meant to rebuild his property, and smaller residential clients out tens of thousands of dollars each — with unfinished sites and no refund. The day after that investigation ran, Maine State Police arrested Brown on a Class B theft warrant and two Class C counts of negotiating a worthless instrument after he wrote $14,500 in bad checks to a lender called Knox Capital, and he admitted to police that he "knew he was passing bad checks" but had "lost control of his business." In January 2025 a Maine judge found Brown liable for $2.5 million in restitution across the civil claims against him and permanently barred him from doing excavation or construction work in Maine, and the Maine Attorney General\'s Office followed with a February 2025 consent judgment against Brown, JBRH, and GGB Property Maintenance; because Brown had nowhere near that much in recoverable assets, the actual settlement locked him into paying just $350,000 back to the state over 15 years in $1,000 monthly installments — a small fraction of what victims were owed. Brown subsequently relocated to Anchorage, Alaska, where he was arrested again in mid-2025 on an outstanding Maine warrant, though Maine chose not to pay extradition costs to bring him back, leaving the underlying criminal theft and worthless-instrument charges unresolved as of the settlement. The case illustrates a durable weak point in home-improvement contracting: Maine, like most states, does not license general contractors, so a homeowner has no state credential to check before hiring one — the Attorney General\'s Office instead urges getting a written, staged-payment contract that ties each installment to a completed phase of work, checking a contractor\'s history through the Consumer Protection Division\'s formal-actions archive and local small-claims records before paying anything upfront, and treating a demand for full or near-full payment before work starts as a serious warning sign on its own.',
    categorySlug: 'home-improvement-solar',
    country: 'US',
    state: 'ME',
    isHistorical: true,
    firstRecorded: '2022-01-01',
    sources: ['Maine Office of the Attorney General — Consumer Protection Division', 'Bangor Daily News'],
    sourceUrl: 'https://digitalmaine.com/ag_consumer_division_formal_actions/799/',
