    slug: 'massachusetts-unitedhealthcare-masshealth-senior-care-options-fraud',
    description: `Massachusetts Attorney General Andrea Joy Campbell filed a lawsuit in Suffolk Superior Court on May 29, 2026 against UnitedHealthcare Insurance Company, alleging the company defrauded MassHealth, the state's Medicaid program, of at least $100 million by falsely inflating the health status of members enrolled in its Senior Care Options (SCO) plan for low-income seniors. Under Senior Care Options, MassHealth pays a higher rate for enrollees whose in-home clinical assessment shows more serious needs, sorted into tiers running from Level 1 (least serious, lowest rate) to Level 3 (most serious, highest rate); the complaint alleges United classified members as Level 2 — reserved for those with a diagnosed behavioral health or substance use disorder — even when they had no corresponding diagnosis or treatment, separately billed for Level 3 designations members did not qualify for, and submitted assessments claiming members needed daily skilled-nursing services most did not actually require or receive, over a period stretching from 2015 through 2025. "The state's managed care plans need to act in good faith on behalf of their members and the financial resources of our state's Medicaid program," Campbell said. UnitedHealthcare has called the complaint "meritless," saying it "doesn't accurately describe our Senior Care Options program, which helps seniors with complex care meet their individual health needs." The case is a reminder that it is a managed-care plan's own clinical assessment — not simply what gets billed — that is supposed to determine the taxpayer-funded rate the state pays on a member's behalf, and Massachusetts seniors or family members who believe a plan's paperwork misrepresents their actual health needs or care received can report it to the Attorney General's Medicaid Fraud Division.`,
