    slug: 'kansas-mgc-construction-tree-trimming-elder-fraud',
    description: `Kansas Attorney General Kris Kobach sued M.GC Construction, LLC — a Missouri-based traveling contractor — along with owner Nathan Leavy and employees Devin Leavy, Wesley Wishon, and Cory Kreigling on February 25, 2026 in Riley County District Court, alleging the crew went door-to-door in Manhattan, Kansas in early 2025 soliciting tree-trimming work without the municipal permits the city required, using aggressive and deceptive sales tactics specifically aimed at elderly residents. In one case cited in the petition, a 75-year-old consumer was charged $31,000 for tree work a local arborist later valued at roughly $4,500 — nearly a 600% markup. The state alleges the defendants pressured customers into writing checks payable to an individual employee rather than the business itself, letting the crew cash the checks immediately at local banks before a customer could issue a stop-payment order, and that they failed to give the written and verbal notice of the three-day cancellation right the Kansas Door-to-Door Sales Act requires for any unsolicited home-sales contract. "My office will not tolerate predatory businesses that target our senior citizens," Kobach said. On May 12, 2026, the Attorney General's office announced judgments entered against all defendants permanently banning them from door-to-door sales or any business activity in Kansas, with five of the six agreeing to a consent judgment requiring full restitution to victims and reimbursement of the state's investigative costs, on top of a $260,000 civil penalty. Homeowners approached by an unsolicited traveling tree or landscaping crew should ask to see a copy of the required city permit before any work begins, get a written estimate first, pay only by a check or method traceable to the actual business name, and remember that Kansas law gives three days to cancel any door-to-door sales contract in writing.`,
